<?xml version="1.0"?>
{{# Source of unraid/bunkarr.xml (the ONE public Community Applications template).                  }}
{{# Edit this file and unraid/ca/publish.env, then run: make ca-template (renders and validates).   }}
{{# Lines starting with the comment marker are dropped; placeholders: see unraid/ca/render.sh.      }}
{{# CA/dockerMan format rules (checked by unraid/ca/validate-template.sh):                          }}
{{#   one Config element per line; write &amp; for &; no angle brackets in text; ASCII only;        }}
{{#   no square brackets in Overview or Changes (CA turns them into tags and strips them);          }}
{{#   Overview: plain text or inline Markdown, a line break only at a paragraph end, no indent;     }}
{{#   write arr, never *arr (an odd number of asterisks starts Markdown emphasis);                  }}
{{#   no TZ entry: Unraid injects TZ itself and an empty TZ would override the server time zone.    }}
{{# The Overview states the data-safety boundaries (validate-template.sh fails without them): Beta, }}
{{#   read-only sources that are never changed, bunkarr.key backed up with the database, and no     }}
{{#   restore wizard yet (manifest-only content is re-acquired by hand until Phase 5 ships it).     }}
{{# Port, mounts, modes, variables, host name and stop timeout mirror deploy/docker-compose.yml:    }}
{{#   deploy/unraid_test.go fails when the two drift apart. Host paths stay empty except /config:   }}
{{#   Unraid creates missing host folders, so a guessed path would create an empty share.           }}
{{# Every release: add its entry at the top of Changes (### X.Y.Z (YYYY-MM-DD), user-facing lines)  }}
{{#   and set VERSION/RELEASE_DATE in publish.env to it; deploy/unraid_changes_test.go checks both  }}
{{#   against the newest release in CHANGELOG.md. CA shows Changes as the app's change log.         }}
<!--
  Bunkarr Docker template for Unraid (dockerMan and Community Applications, template version 2).
  GENERATED by make ca-template from unraid/ca/bunkarr.xml.tmpl with {{ENV_NAME}}.
  Edit those files, not this one. Install and settings: unraid/README.md.
-->
<Container version="2">
  <Name>bunkarr</Name>
  <Repository>{{IMAGE}}</Repository>
  <Registry>{{REGISTRY_URL}}</Registry>
  <Network>bridge</Network>
  <Shell>sh</Shell>
  <Privileged>false</Privileged>
  <Support>{{SUPPORT_URL}}</Support>
  <Project>{{PUBLIC_REPO_URL}}</Project>
  <ReadMe>{{README_URL}}</ReadMe>
  <Overview>Bunkarr is your library's bunker: a self-hosted, arr-style backup app for Plex media libraries and the arr stack. It knows which media can be re-downloaded. Irreplaceable data (the Plex database, the Sonarr, Radarr and Lidarr configuration and databases, personal media, rare content) gets full, versioned backups. Common, easily re-acquired content can get a manifest-only backup instead (TMDB, TVDB and IMDb IDs, quality profile, root folder, path): a list from which Sonarr or Radarr can download it again. Your tier rules decide which content that is; until you add rules, everything is copied in full. Bunkarr never modifies or deletes your source media: the media, Plex and arr folders are mounted read-only.

It mirrors your libraries to a mounted share (a NAS over NFS or SMB mounted with Unassigned Devices, or another disk) and, encrypted, to off-site storage (Backblaze B2, any S3-compatible service or an SFTP server) through restic or rclone, with a recovery kit that restores without Bunkarr. It backs up the Plex database while Plex runs and the Sonarr, Radarr and Lidarr configuration, backs up an arr import within a minute through its webhook, writes manifests of every arr item, keeps replaced and deleted files for 30 days, verifies backups by reading them back, and holds unusually large deletes for you to confirm.

Beta, pre-1.0. Arr-style web UI and API on port 8787: create your login right after the first start. Keep the appdata folder on a local pool and back up bunkarr.key together with the database (bunkarr.db): without the key the stored credentials cannot be decrypted, and Bunkarr does not back up its own database. The restore wizard is not built yet: restores are file copies or the restic and rclone commands of the recovery kit, and re-acquiring manifest-only content is manual (each manifest lists the IDs, root folder and quality profile to add an item back in Sonarr or Radarr).</Overview>
  <Category>Backup: MediaApp:Video MediaApp:Music</Category>
  <WebUI>http://[IP]:[PORT:8787]/</WebUI>
  <TemplateURL>{{TEMPLATE_URL}}</TemplateURL>
  <Icon>{{ICON_URL}}</Icon>
  <ExtraParams>--hostname=bunkarr-tower --stop-timeout=60</ExtraParams>
  <Requires>Extra Parameters (Advanced View): keep --hostname=bunkarr-tower, with tower replaced by this server's name if it differs. Every install needs its own host name that never changes: restic uses it to tell stale repository locks apart. Settings - Docker - Docker Stop Timeout: raise it to 60 seconds. Unraid's own stops ignore the --stop-timeout=60 in Extra Parameters and kill a container after that timeout (10 seconds by default); a job killed three times in a row fails. PUID and PGID: the user Plex runs as (99 and 100 on Unraid), so Bunkarr can read Plex's Preferences.xml. A backup share mounted with Unassigned Devices needs access mode Read/Write - Slave. Plex Media Server, Sonarr, Radarr and Lidarr are optional.</Requires>
  <ExtraSearchTerms>backup plex sonarr radarr lidarr arr restic rclone b2 s3 sftp offsite versioning manifest nas</ExtraSearchTerms>
  <License>GPL-3.0-or-later</License>
  <Beta>true</Beta>
  <MinVer>6.12</MinVer>
  <Date>{{RELEASE_DATE}}</Date>
  <Changes>### 0.1.0 (2026-10-01)&#xD;
- First release, and the first in Community Applications.&#xD;
- Mirrors your libraries to a mounted share and, encrypted, to Backblaze B2, S3-compatible storage or SFTP through restic or rclone, with a recovery kit.&#xD;
- Plex database backup while Plex runs; Sonarr, Radarr and Lidarr configuration backups, webhooks and manifests; tier rules for full or manifest-only backups.&#xD;
- Beta: the restore wizard is not built yet, so restores and re-acquiring manifest-only content are manual.</Changes>
  <Config Name="WebUI Port" Target="8787" Default="8787" Mode="tcp" Description="Web UI, API and the Sonarr, Radarr and Lidarr webhooks. Readarr also uses 8787 by default: if it runs on this server, change only the host port here (for example to 8788) and use that port in the arr webhook URLs." Type="Port" Display="always" Required="true" Mask="false">8787</Config>
  <Config Name="Appdata" Target="/config" Default="/mnt/user/appdata/bunkarr" Mode="rw" Description="Bunkarr's database (bunkarr.db), bunkarr.key, logs, Plex DB staging (free space for one copy of the Plex database) and the restic cache. Keep it on a local pool or cache disk, never on a network share. Back up bunkarr.key together with bunkarr.db (for example with the Appdata Backup plugin): without the key the stored credentials cannot be decrypted." Type="Path" Display="always" Required="true" Mask="false">/mnt/user/appdata/bunkarr</Config>
  <Config Name="Media" Target="/media" Default="" Mode="ro" Description="Your library, read-only: Bunkarr never modifies or deletes source media. Map the ONE folder that holds both downloads and library (TRaSH layout: /mnt/user/data) so hardlinks are detected, and turn on Settings - Global Share Settings - Tunable (support Hard Links). Media on an Unassigned Devices disk or share needs access mode Read Only - Slave. Unraid creates a missing path as an empty folder, so check it." Type="Path" Display="always" Required="true" Mask="false"></Config>
  <Config Name="Plex data" Target="/plex" Default="" Mode="ro" Description="Optional, for the Plex database backup: Plex's Plex Media Server data folder (the one holding Preferences.xml and Plug-in Support), read-only: the Plex container's appdata folder plus /Library/Application Support/Plex Media Server. For linuxserver's container (plex) that is /mnt/user/appdata/plex/Library/Application Support/Plex Media Server, for the official one (Plex-Media-Server) /mnt/user/appdata/Plex-Media-Server/Library/Application Support/Plex Media Server. Check that the path exists: Unraid creates a missing one as an empty folder. Then set Data path /plex in Bunkarr (Settings - Plex)." Type="Path" Display="always" Required="false" Mask="false"></Config>
  <Config Name="Backup destination" Target="/backup" Default="" Mode="rw,slave" Description="A backup destination as mounted on the host, read-write: a NAS share mounted with Unassigned Devices (for example /mnt/remotes/NAS_backup; NFS recommended) or another disk. Keep Read/Write - Slave: Unassigned Devices mounts shares after Docker starts, and without slave propagation Bunkarr sees the empty mount point and refuses to sync. In Bunkarr add a destination with target /backup or a folder inside it. Off-site-only setups (B2, S3, SFTP) still need a folder here, which Bunkarr then leaves unused." Type="Path" Display="always" Required="true" Mask="false"></Config>
  <Config Name="Sonarr Backups folder" Target="/arr/sonarr-backups" Default="" Mode="ro" Description="Optional: Sonarr's Backups folder (its /config/Backups, for example /mnt/user/appdata/sonarr/Backups), read-only, for Sonarr configuration backups when Sonarr requires a login (its API key cannot download them then). The folder must exist first: run System - Backup - Backup Now in Sonarr once before you set this (Unraid creates a missing path as an empty folder, its parent folders owned by root). Then set Backups folder /arr/sonarr-backups in Bunkarr (Settings - Connect - Sonarr - Backup)." Type="Path" Display="advanced" Required="false" Mask="false"></Config>
  <Config Name="Radarr Backups folder" Target="/arr/radarr-backups" Default="" Mode="ro" Description="Optional: Radarr's Backups folder (its /config/Backups, for example /mnt/user/appdata/radarr/Backups), read-only, for Radarr configuration backups when Radarr requires a login (its API key cannot download them then). The folder must exist first: run System - Backup - Backup Now in Radarr once before you set this (Unraid creates a missing path as an empty folder, its parent folders owned by root). Then set Backups folder /arr/radarr-backups in Bunkarr (Settings - Connect - Radarr - Backup)." Type="Path" Display="advanced" Required="false" Mask="false"></Config>
  <Config Name="Lidarr Backups folder" Target="/arr/lidarr-backups" Default="" Mode="ro" Description="Optional: Lidarr's Backups folder (its /config/Backups, for example /mnt/user/appdata/lidarr/Backups), read-only, for Lidarr configuration backups when Lidarr requires a login (its API key cannot download them then). The folder must exist first: run System - Backup - Backup Now in Lidarr once before you set this (Unraid creates a missing path as an empty folder, its parent folders owned by root). Then set Backups folder /arr/lidarr-backups in Bunkarr (Settings - Connect - Lidarr - Backup)." Type="Path" Display="advanced" Required="false" Mask="false"></Config>
  <Config Name="PUID" Target="PUID" Default="99" Mode="" Description="User id Bunkarr runs as: the user Plex runs as (Unraid: 99, nobody), so Bunkarr can read Plex's Preferences.xml, which Plex keeps at mode 0600. It must also be able to read your media and the arr Backups folders and write the backup share." Type="Variable" Display="advanced" Required="true" Mask="false">99</Config>
  <Config Name="PGID" Target="PGID" Default="100" Mode="" Description="Group id Bunkarr runs as: the group Plex runs as (Unraid: 100, users)." Type="Variable" Display="advanced" Required="true" Mask="false">100</Config>
  <Config Name="UMASK" Target="UMASK" Default="022" Mode="" Description="umask for the files and folders Bunkarr writes (022: files 0644, folders 0755; 002 keeps them group-writable). bunkarr.key, the Plex Preferences.xml copies and the arr backup zips are always 0600." Type="Variable" Display="advanced" Required="false" Mask="false">022</Config>
  <Config Name="Log level" Target="BUNKARR_LOG_LEVEL" Default="info|debug|warn|error" Mode="" Description="Detail of the container log and of /config/logs/bunkarr.log (always JSON). Use debug for support requests." Type="Variable" Display="advanced" Required="false" Mask="false">info</Config>
</Container>
